an3.57:6.1 starts: Among cattle of any sort,
an3.57:6.2 starts: whether black, white, red, or golden,
an3.57:6.3 starts: mottled, uniform, or pigeon-colored,
an3.57:6.4 none
an3.57:7.3 starts: the one that can bear the load,
an3.57:7.4 starts: possessing strength, advancing with good speed.
an4.10:10.3 starts: But having entirely understood
an4.10:10.4 starts: sense pleasures and the bond of existence,
an4.11:11.1 starts: But one who, whether walking,
an4.11:11.2 starts: standing, sitting, or lying down,
an4.12:8.1 starts: Training in what is conducive
an4.12:8.2 starts: to serenity of mind, always mindful,
an4.12:8.3 starts: they call such a bhikkhu
an4.12:8.4 starts: one constantly resolute.
an4.14:5.3 starts: these four strivings were taught
an4.14:5.4 starts: by the Kinsman of the Sun.
an4.16:2.5 starts: having known volitional activities
an4.16:2.6 starts: as alien, as suffering, and not as
an4.17:2.1 starts: If through desire, hate, fear, or delusion
an4.17:2.2 starts: one transgresses against the Dhamma,
an4.17:2.3 starts: one’s fame diminishes like the moon
an4.17:2.4 starts: in the dark fortnight.
an4.18:2.1 starts: If one does not transgress the Dhamma
an4.18:2.2 starts: through desire, hate, fear, or delusion,
an4.18:2.3 starts: one’s fame becomes full like the moon
an4.18:2.4 starts: in the bright fortnight.
